                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9d4e606d-fb40-373b-bfed-2a1ebcf327f8",
                        "entry_id": "6dc5b967-c82d-361e-b0be-1e10d5100be8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Chacune des ailes que, selon la mythologie, Hermès/Mercure portait aux talons."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "49a8023c-582a-4444-871f-253842907146",
                                "sense_id": "9d4e606d-fb40-373b-bfed-2a1ebcf327f8",
                                "text": "Il reçut avant de partir des ailes et des talonnières, ainsi que le Pétase, dont lui fit présent Mercure, pour l’aider à traverser les airs. Vulcain l’arma d’un harpê, sabre recourbé, d’un métal très-dur."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "47678f1f-99f2-44b7-8e56-62330e55b667",
                        "source_entry_id": "6dc5b967-c82d-361e-b0be-1e10d5100be8",
                        "source_sense_id": "9d4e606d-fb40-373b-bfed-2a1ebcf327f8",
                        "target_lang_word": "talaria",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "talaria",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "eab1b78d-4da9-3c1a-abdb-02d18def9b88",
                                        "entry_id": "2883028f-29a7-3859-a91a-c6a3eb5e1f0c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The winged sandals worn by certain gods and goddesses, especially the Roman god Mercury (and his Greek counterpart Hermes)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Greek",
                                            "Roman",
                                            "plural",
                                            "plural-only"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cb459578-1a2f-421a-bdc1-558ba0033799",
                                                "sense_id": "eab1b78d-4da9-3c1a-abdb-02d18def9b88",
                                                "text": "Byres has drawn these figures with wings at their ankles, sometimes fastened to the leg, and sometimes like those at their shoulders, growing from the flesh—in both which ways the talaria of Mercury and Perseus are represented on ancient monuments. Nothing of this sort could I perceive; it was manifest to me that these were not talaria, but simple buskins with peaked flaps, such as are commonly depicted on vases of the archaic Greek style, and on the legs of Roman Lares in the paintings of Pompeii."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "85ea1b3a-5b83-4638-b2e8-db99620c0b17",
                                                "sense_id": "eab1b78d-4da9-3c1a-abdb-02d18def9b88",
                                                "text": "From the Graiæ the literary myths make him [Perseus] go to the Nymphs, the artistic ones to the Naiads or nymphs of the lake Tritonis, from which he receives the helmet of Hades, the kibisis or wallet, and the talaria or winged sandals. [...] Other versions made him receive the helmet and talaria from Hermes. On the early monuments the helmet of Hades is like the petasus of Hermes, and the sandals are winged, but in many other works of art, such as mirrors or gems, the talaria are represented as wings, which he attaches to his feet."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "efaa5be0-e75a-46e1-a190-c00df0c77d8c",
                                                "sense_id": "eab1b78d-4da9-3c1a-abdb-02d18def9b88",
                                                "text": "With his horizon all his own, yet he a poor man, born to be poor, with his inherited Irish poverty or poor life, his Adam's grandmother and boggy ways, not to rise in this world, he nor his posterity, till their wading webbed bog-trotting feet get talaria to their heels."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8272d7c7-ea6d-4b7d-9df4-658375630f29",
                                                "sense_id": "eab1b78d-4da9-3c1a-abdb-02d18def9b88",
                                                "text": "It was the god Hermes, he who conducted souls to the Elysian fields, the slender, agile, elegant figure, beautiful in its sinuous motion, with the petasus on his head and the winged talaria on his ankles, that I beheld floating over me."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "word_linkages": []
            }
